### A Simple Example: Updating the Document Title

Let's build a simple counter that updates the browser tab's title every time the count changes.

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Counter() {
  // 1. Set up our state
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // 2. Set up our effect
  useEffect(() => {
    // This code runs AFTER the component renders
    document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
  }, [count]); // <-- The Dependency Array

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Click me
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Counter;
```

### Breaking it Down

1. **The Setup (`useEffect(...)`)**: We pass a function to `useEffect`. This function contains our side effect (changing the document title).
2. **The Dependency Array (`[count]`)**: This is the crucial part. It tells React: *"Only run this effect if the `count` variable has changed."* 
